faq:
  - q: Are psychodynamic therapy and psychoanalysis the same thing?
    a: They share the same roots. Psychodynamic psychotherapy is based on psychoanalytic thinking, which began with Freud and was later extended by many others. Today it is practised in forms that differ from psychoanalysis and is adapted to the person's needs.
  - q: How many sessions does psychodynamic therapy take?
    a: We do not give a number of sessions in advance; the length of the process depends on the individual's needs and the issue being worked on. Sessions last 50 minutes and usually take place once a week or once every two weeks. Where things stand is reviewed together at regular points.
  - q: Do I have to talk about my childhood in psychodynamic therapy?
    a: No. The work often starts from the present. The past comes into the conversation when it helps to understand the present and when you are ready. You decide what to share and when.
  - q: Why doesn't the therapist talk much about themselves?
    a: In psychodynamic work the therapist keeps their attention on what you bring and generally keeps their own personal feelings in the background. This distance is not coldness; it makes room for the feelings and expectations you bring to therapy to become more visible.
  - q: Are dreams discussed in psychodynamic therapy?
    a: They can be. Dreams, fantasies and memories can come into the work as part of your inner world. But what gets talked about in a session is set by what is on your mind that day, not by an agenda.
  - q: Can psychodynamic therapy be done online?
    a: Yes. At our centre, sessions can take place in person or online, in Turkish or English. Which format suits you is decided together in the first meeting.
