blocks:
  - type: metin
    baslik: Defining psychodynamic psychotherapy
    metin: |-
      The American Psychological Association (APA) describes psychoanalysis and psychodynamic therapies as approaches that focus on changing problematic behaviours, feelings and thoughts by discovering their unconscious meanings and motivations. These therapies involve a close working partnership between therapist and individual; people learn about themselves by exploring their interactions in the therapeutic relationship ([APA](https://www.apa.org/topics/psychotherapy/approaches)).

      An everyday example: whenever a partner seems to pull back a little, you may feel an intense fear of being left, and each time it ends with you being the one who ends the relationship. You may even have a sensible explanation: "It was never going to work anyway." Psychodynamic work is curious about the feeling beneath that reaction, one that has not yet been named, and about where it comes from.
  - type: metin
    baslik: Where does psychodynamic therapy come from?
    metin: |-
      As Tavistock and Portman NHS Foundation Trust explains, psychodynamic psychotherapy has its roots in psychoanalysis, which began with the work of Sigmund Freud in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries. Freud developed a theory of the mind based on the idea that unconscious thoughts, feelings and conflicts shape human behaviour. Later figures such as Anna Freud, Melanie Klein, Donald Winnicott and John Bowlby developed the field further, towards a broader understanding of the mind centred on relationships ([Tavistock and Portman](https://tavistockandportman.ac.uk/articles/what-is-psychodynamic-psychotherapy/)).

      Psychodynamic psychotherapy as practised today builds on this body of work. It shares its roots with psychoanalysis, but it is not a direct application of Freud's original model.
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: What does psychodynamic psychotherapy work with?
    giris: A few core ideas of the approach also explain what gets talked about in sessions.
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Unconscious processes
        metin: We are not fully aware of some of our feelings, wishes and fears. Even so, they can influence our choices, who we get close to and what we avoid. The work aims to bring these hidden thoughts and feelings into a form that can be talked about.
      - baslik: The inner world
        metin: The European Federation for Psychoanalytic Psychotherapy (EFPP) describes psychoanalytic psychotherapy as deepening understanding of the individual's internal world, including inner conflicts and relationships, emotions, fantasies, dreams, memories, ideals and thoughts ([EFPP](https://www.efpp.org/)).
      - baslik: Recurring relationship patterns
        metin: 'The sense of "I always end up in the same place" is one of the common starting points for psychodynamic work. Relationships with different people that end in similar ways may show that ways of being close and keeping distance learned early on are still at work today.'
      - baslik: The therapeutic relationship and transference
        metin: How the person relates to the therapist, and the roles they assign to the therapist at different times, also become part of the conversation. This is called transference. What happens in relationships outside can appear in the therapy room too, which makes it possible to look at it together in a safe setting ([Tavistock and Portman](https://tavistockandportman.ac.uk/articles/what-is-psychodynamic-psychotherapy/)).
  - type: onayli-liste
    baslik: What happens in a psychodynamic therapy session?
    giris: 'Psychodynamic sessions are less structured than CBT sessions. In practice, this means:'
    maddeler:
      - baslik: There is no set agenda
        aciklama: You are encouraged to talk freely about whatever is on your mind that day, even if it seems unimportant or unrelated.
      - baslik: The therapist listens and notices connections
        aciklama: The psychologist notices recurring themes and emotional patterns in what you say and makes sense of them together with you.
      - baslik: The frame stays steady
        aciklama: Sessions take place at regular times in a confidential setting. This continuity provides a basis of trust for talking about difficult feelings.
    kapanis: Sessions last 50 minutes and usually take place once a week or once every two weeks. The length of the process depends on the individual's needs; no number of sessions is given in advance, and where things stand is reviewed together at regular points.
  - type: metin
    baslik: How psychodynamic therapy differs from CBT
    metin: |-
      In [cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT)](/en/blog/what-is-cognitive-behavioural-therapy.html), sessions follow an agenda and there are small experiments and records between sessions. In psychodynamic therapy the weight is on talking. Early experiences may be discussed to understand the roots of a current difficulty ([APA](https://www.apa.org/topics/psychotherapy/understanding)).

      This does not mean one is better than the other; the two approaches focus on different questions. CBT asks, "Which thoughts and behaviours are keeping this cycle going right now?" Psychodynamic therapy is interested in, "Why does this cycle keep repeating, and what does it answer to in me?" Many psychologists are not tied to a single approach either, and draw on different approaches to shape the work around the person.
  - type: onayli-liste
    baslik: What is psychodynamic psychotherapy used for?
    giris: 'These are the areas we often work on with a psychodynamic approach at our centre:'
    maddeler:
      - baslik: Recurring relationship patterns and relationships that end in similar ways
      - baslik: '[Long-standing low mood](/en/blog/what-is-depression.html)'
      - baslik: Identity, self-worth and "who am I" questions
      - baslik: Unresolved grief and loss
      - baslik: A sense of emptiness or dissatisfaction that is hard to name
  - type: metin
    baslik: Psychodynamic psychotherapy at our centre
    metin: |-
      The work is shaped around each individual's life story and needs, drawing on other approaches where helpful. Details of how we work with this approach are on the [psychodynamic therapy](/en/services/psychodynamic-therapy.html) page.

      In the first meeting we talk about what brought you here, your expectations and what kind of work might suit you. If you would like to know what to expect beforehand, see our article on [what to know before starting therapy](/en/blog/before-starting-therapy.html) and our [therapy process](/en/therapy-process.html) page. Sessions can take place in Turkish or English, at our centre in Famagusta or [online](/en/services/online-therapy.html). For any questions, you can [get in touch](/en/contact.html).
